1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,214
One narrowed valve. Two different ways to
replace it.

2
00:00:04,725 --> 00:00:09,427
Both procedures aim to let blood leave the
heart through a new aortic valve.

3
00:00:10,031 --> 00:00:14,663
The Heart Team weighs how each option fits
the person, both now and in the years

4
00:00:14,710 --> 00:00:15,348
ahead.

5
00:00:15,952 --> 00:00:20,143
TAVI is transcatheter aortic valve
implantation.

6
00:00:20,840 --> 00:00:25,240
The team delivers a replacement valve
through a catheter, often from an artery

7
00:00:25,298 --> 00:00:30,429
in the groin. In the illustrated case, the
frame expands inside the

8
00:00:30,499 --> 00:00:33,854
narrowed valve and holds its old leaflets
aside.

9
00:00:34,365 --> 00:00:37,570
The replacement leaflets then control
blood flow.

10
00:00:38,173 --> 00:00:41,529
Surgical aortic valve replacement takes a
different route.

11
00:00:42,039 --> 00:00:47,008
The surgeon typically removes the diseased
valve and puts a new valve in its place.

12
00:00:47,705 --> 00:00:52,709
Depending on the person and the operation,
surgical options can include a tissue or a

13
00:00:52,767 --> 00:00:57,098
mechanical valve. Both can be assessed
against the patient’s long-term

14
00:00:57,156 --> 00:00:58,178
priorities.

15
00:00:58,688 --> 00:01:03,565
The team considers the size and shape of
the valve, where calcium has built up, and

16
00:01:03,611 --> 00:01:06,259
whether the blood vessels can carry the
catheter.

17
00:01:06,955 --> 00:01:10,543
It also asks whether another heart problem
needs attention.

18
00:01:11,146 --> 00:01:15,999
For example, some patients who need
coronary bypass or another valve operation

19
00:01:16,243 --> 00:01:18,484
may benefit from a surgical plan.

20
00:01:19,296 --> 00:01:23,488
Catheter-based treatment often allows a
shorter early recovery.

21
00:01:23,998 --> 00:01:28,294
Surgery may be more suitable for some
people even when recovery takes longer.

22
00:01:28,898 --> 00:01:33,472
The team discusses the risks each person
faces, including complications such as

23
00:01:33,565 --> 00:01:36,293
bleeding, stroke, or changes in heart
rhythm.

24
00:01:36,502 --> 00:01:39,591
It also asks what support the person will
have at home.

25
00:01:40,032 --> 00:01:43,967
The team considers how long the
replacement valve is expected to work, the

26
00:01:44,026 --> 00:01:48,078
person’s overall health and life
expectancy, and what future care could

27
00:01:48,113 --> 00:01:52,919
involve. If another procedure is needed,
the first valve may affect the next

28
00:01:52,989 --> 00:01:58,318
options. The team weighs those questions
alongside the risks and recovery of the

29
00:01:58,376 --> 00:01:59,339
first treatment.

30
00:01:59,943 --> 00:02:04,389
The Heart Team reviews the evidence and
explains why it recommends an option.

31
00:02:04,993 --> 00:02:09,149
The patient brings their preferences,
goals and questions to that decision.

32
00:02:09,753 --> 00:02:14,316
In our next series, we’ll look at cardiac
surgery, beginning with why a team might

33
00:02:14,374 --> 00:02:19,076
recommend coronary artery bypass and how a
graft restores a route for blood

34
00:02:19,122 --> 00:02:19,831
flow.
